                             User Management

*types of users in linux:
1. root user:
--> root user is a super user in system.who has all the access pass to the 
system functinality.

2. Standard user:
--> standard users are those users which are created by root and they have 
limited permission compared to root user.

3. System user:
--> system user is a user account created by the system who
used to run background processes and services.

adduser <user_name> / useradd <user_name>
--> to create user
adduser seema

deluser <user_name>
--> to delete user
deluser seema

**Important files:
/etc/passwd = all user information stored
/etc/shells = store all system supported shell information
/etc/shadow = store all user password related information
/etc/group = store all group related information

/etc/passwd contains:
seema  :  x  :  1000  :  1000  :  ,,,  :  /home/seema  :  /bin/bash
  1       2       3       4        5          6               7

  1 : user name
  2 : user account password
  3 : user ID --> UID
  4 : group ID
  5 : user other details
  6 : user home directory
  7 : shell type

  usermod -u <new_uid> <user_name>
  --> to change user ID
  usermod -u 7001 seema

  usermod -g <new_gid> <user_name>
  --> to change group ID
  usermod -g 1001 seema

  usermod -c "<commment>" <user_name>
  --> to modify user information
  usermod -c "seema patil, 0123456789, Mumbai" seema

  usermod -d <new_homedir> <user_name>
  --> to change user working directory
  usermod -d /mnt seema

  usermod -s <new_shell> <user_name>
  --> to change shell type
  usermod -s /bin/sh seema

  usermod -l <old_name> <new_name> 
  --> to rename user name
  usermod -l seema pooja

  passwd <user_name>
  --> to change user account passsword 
  passwd seema 

  usermod -L <user_name>
  --> to lock user account
  usermod -L seema

  usermod -U <user_name>
  --> to  unlock user account
  usermod -U seema


